WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.800
Credential storage design starts with the user table or identity store.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.860
The system should store a password verifier, not the password itself.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:18.400
Useful fields often include the encoded password hash, algorithm identifier, parameter metadata, version information, timestamps, and account state needed for authentication workflows.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:25.020
The goal is to store enough information to verify and migrate credentials without storing unnecessary credential material.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.480
Version metadata supports migration.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:32.580
Over time, teams may move from one algorithm to another or increase cost parameters.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:45.040
Rehashing on login is a common strategy: after a successful authentication, the system detects that the stored verifier uses older parameters and replaces it with a new verifier generated from the presented password.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:50.060
This allows gradual migration without forcing every user through a reset on the same day.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.200
Credential databases and backups should be protected as sensitive assets.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:58.820
Access should be limited to the services and administrators that truly need it.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:06.320
Backups should receive the same or stronger protection as the primary store because an attacker with old hashes can still attempt offline guessing.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:13.420
Analytics exports, support tools, and reporting queries should avoid copying credential fields into secondary systems.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.040
Design should assume compromise is possible.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:21.300
If the database is stolen, unique salts and strong password hashing slow offline attacks.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.920
If the application secret is also exposed, pepper-based protections may be lost.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.620
If logs contain plaintext passwords, hashing no longer helps those users.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:39.880
Credential storage architecture is therefore a layered design: minimize stored material, protect access, support migration, and plan for breach response.
